d has seen influential attention by Garsia-Procesi, Garsia-Haiman, N.\nThe \"strong\" parking conjecture asserts that there is a $W \\times C$-equivariant bijection between $\\mathrm{Park}^{\\mathrm{NC}}_W$ and $Z_W$. <br><br> The orbit harmonics technique was introduced by Kostant and has seen influential attention by Garsia-Procesi, Garsia-Haiman, N. Bergeron-Gagnon, and others. It has played a hugely important role in my research, leading to to quotient rings for delta operators, quantum Donaldson-Thomas invariants, the Viennot shadow avatar of RSK, and more. I am forever grateful to Catalan theory for introducing me to this remarkable technique. >$~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~$Brendon Rhoades </div> <center><!--<img data-src=\"./img/Gordon, Griffeth - Title.png\" height=\"300\">$~~~~~$<img data-src=\"./img/Etingof - Title.png\" height=\"300\">$~~~~~$<img data-src=\"./img/Griffeth - Title.png\" height=\"300\">$~~~~~$<img data-src=\"./img/Cartaya, Griffeth - Title.png\" height=\"300\">--></center> ### <u>Rational noncrossing parking functions</u> > **Thm.** (Galashin, Lam, Trinh, Williams) Fix $W$ an irreducible Coxeter group with standard Coxeter element $c=s_1s_2\\cdots s_r$.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nConjectures | trureturing (https://www.trureturing.org/conjectures)\nciteturn0search3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Discrete structures Equivalence proved First preprint 2026 Why do two parking rules lead to the same count? ... The question Can circular parking with two choices per car be matched explicitly to classical parking functions, while keeping the prescribed vacancy?\n\nRelease source record Proved in Lean\n\nOpen problem\n\n### A canonical fixed-fiber bijection for circular two-choice parking\n\nOpen Problem 1 of the linked paper asks:\n\n